{% doc %}
  The class list for a reading-width wrapper.

  ## Not the same thing as a container

  A container decides how far content sits from the edge of the *screen*. This
  decides how long a *line of text* runs before it wraps. They are independent:
  a rich-text section can sit in a wide container and still hold its paragraphs
  to a readable measure, which is exactly what you want when the section has a
  background that should span the page.

  Because of that they use different mechanisms, and it is worth knowing which
  is which when reading `base.css`:

    container      padding-inline, so the box still spans the viewport and a
                   child can bleed out to the edge
    content-width  max-inline-size, because a measure is about the text, not
                   the viewport

  Sized in `ch` — characters — so the value tracks whatever font the merchant
  picked instead of a pixel guess that goes wrong the moment they switch to a
  wider typeface. The three measures are constants in `assets/base.css`, not
  theme settings: they are something a store sets once, and three sliders that
  can be dragged out of order is a worse control than three named steps.

  ## Alignment

  Centred by default, which is what a standalone rich-text block wants. `start`
  and `end` move the free space instead of the box, so the measure still holds;
  they are for text set alongside an image, where centring inside the column
  reads as a mistake.

  Parameters:
    measure (string)  narrow | standard | wide | full. Defaults to standard.
    align   (string)  start | center | end. Defaults to center.
    classes (string)  Extra classes to append.

  @example
    <div class="{% render 'content-width', measure: 'narrow' %}">

  @example
    <div class="{% render 'content-width', measure: section.settings.content_measure, align: section.settings.alignment %}">
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  case measure
    when 'narrow'
      assign measure_class = 'content-width content-width--narrow'
    when 'wide'
      assign measure_class = 'content-width content-width--wide'
    when 'full'
      assign measure_class = 'content-width content-width--full'
    else
      assign measure_class = 'content-width'
  endcase

  assign out = measure_class

  # `center` is the default and has no class of its own, so an unrecognised
  # value lands there rather than on a class that does not exist.
  case align
    when 'start'
      assign out = out | append: ' content-width--start'
    when 'end'
      assign out = out | append: ' content-width--end'
  endcase

  if classes != blank
    assign out = out | append: ' ' | append: classes
  endif
-%}
{{- out -}}
